  {
    [36mts[39m:        [31m"2026-09-17T10:21:02.123Z"[39m,  [32m// ISO 8601, UTC[39m
    [36mlevel[39m:     [31m"info"[39m,                       [32m// debug | info | warn | error[39m
    [36mmsg[39m:       [31m"login attempt"[39m,              [32m// static string[39m
    [36msessionId[39m: [31m"9f2c..."[39m,                    [32m// ephemeral, per page load / process[39m
    [36menv[39m:       [31m"browser"[39m,                    [32m// or "node"[39m
    [36mctx[39m:       { [32m/* allowlisted keys only */[39m },
    [36merr[39m:       { name, message, stack }      [32m// errors only[39m
  }

  [1mThe one rule that holds the whole redaction story together: [38;5;153mmsg[39m is a static string.[0m All variable data goes in [38;5;153mctx[39m,
  where the allowlist can see it. If [38;5;153mmsg[39m were interpolated — [38;5;153m`Logging in: ${username}`[39m — the deny-by-default allowlist
  would be bypassable by accident in the most natural-looking way possible, and your log store would fill with
  unredacted values that no scrubber ever inspected. Static messages also make records groupable, which is the other
  thing you want during an incident. I'd enforce this by having the logger take [38;5;153m(msg, ctx)[39m and never accepting a
  template, and by lint if we add one.

  Four levels, numerically ordered, with a configurable minimum. Node reads [38;5;153mLOG_LEVEL[39m, defaulting to [38;5;153minfo[39m. The browser
  defaults to [38;5;153mwarn[39m, with [38;5;153mdebug[39m on [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m, and an explicit override the page can set.

  [38;5;153msessionId[39m is a [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m minted once at module load — available natively in both Node 18+ and all current
  browsers, so no dependency and no fallback.

